      "rationale": "Both tests exercise sqlparse.format(..., reindent_aligned=True), the behaviour reported as regressed ('basic' and a GROUP BY variant closely related to the reported 'group by subquery' case, exercising the same AlignedIndentFilter._split_kwds/nl()/by_words machinery). The expected strings are not taken from the current (buggy) output but derived mechanically from AlignedIndentFilter's own documented arithmetic (nl() = max_kwd_len - len(keyword) + indent + offset, with the \"+3\" GROUP/ORDER BY hack in _process_default), which encodes the library's intended invariant that every clause's content starts at a single fixed column (7, or 10 for the GROUP BY block) - the defining property of \"aligned\" reindenting. If the regression breaks this column alignment, the assertions fail now and will pass again once the arithmetic/behaviour is restored.",
