      "code_sha256_lf": "be6d9860bf52ccbac96de88f0bd0ec7458a8b0c93c6c37f85c6dccca35190376",
      "rationale": "AlignedIndentFilter.nl() computes a uniform column width (len('SELECT')+1 = 7 chars) so that content after every top-level clause keyword starts in the same column, and _process_parenthesis/_process_default's \"+3\" GROUP BY hack is documented to keep a subquery's own clause keywords aligned the same way *inside* the parenthesis, independent of where that parenthesis sits in the outer query. I construct a query containing a FROM-subquery followed by an outer GROUP BY (the \"group by subquery\" scenario from the report) and check, line by line, that (a) every outer top-level clause's content starts at column 7, exactly as in the simple case, and (b) inside the subquery the SELECT/FROM content is aligned at a consistent column that is shifted by exactly one indent level (8 columns) relative to the outer alignment, which is what the nl()/offset()/indent() arithmetic in aligned_indent.py guarantees when working correctly. I also keep the plain basic SELECT/FROM/WHERE/AND case from the report as a baseline, asserting the same column-alignment invariant derived directly from the filter's own documented formula rather than from whatever the current buggy code happens to emit.",
